  "narrative": "The new protocol and library probes are reassuring null results. Media serum 10 versus 5 (H131, +0.002 [-0.073,0.076]) and the Humagne CRISPR library (H132, -0.047 [-0.177,0.083]) are not associated with scientifically meaningful dependency_composite differences, so the main tumor-suppressor-loss associations are less likely to be artifacts of broad culture-serum or library heterogeneity. These are dataset-internal negative checks, not proof that unmeasured protocol effects are absent. The co-loss background checks strongly support persistence of the major associations when one other accepted loss is present and other major losses are excluded. KEAP1 loss with SMARCA4 loss present gives +0.738 [0.641,0.834] (H133); KEAP1 loss with TP53 loss present gives +0.963 [0.762,1.164] (H135); TP53 loss with KEAP1 loss present gives +0.549 [0.370,0.727] (H136); SMARCA4 loss with KEAP1 loss present gives +0.887 [0.792,0.982] (H137); and SMARCA4 loss with TP53 loss present gives +0.947 [0.711,1.184] (H138). All have point estimates far above the 0.15 threshold and intervals excluding both null and threshold, so I accept and close them as context-specific dataset associations, not causal epistasis. H134, TP53 loss in a SMARCA4-loss-only clean background, is positive and above the threshold in point estimate (+0.263), but the interval is wide and its lower bound (0.045) is close to null and far below threshold; with only 63 exposed observations in that co-loss cell, I do not accept it as a threshold-clear context effect and keep it unresolved and deferred. PTEN remains the weakest accepted predictor. H126, clean-background high-quality adherent PTEN loss, gives +0.221 [0.110,0.331]; this is consistent with the already validated clean-background adherent PTEN effect (H103) and supports PTEN loss as a modest association with stronger dependency, but the single-context lower bound does not itself exclude the 0.15 threshold. I accept H126 as a replicated-consistent context but keep it active pending independent validation. H127, broad high-quality adherent PTEN loss, is +0.199 [0.061,0.337], also positive but less clean and with a lower bound well below threshold; given overlap with H103, H112, and H126, and the multiplicity risk for near-threshold PTEN results, I keep it unresolved and deferred. The two interaction checks do not support modifier claims: H128 screen-quality interaction is signed opposite to the claimed direction and crosses null, while H120 PIK3CA interaction is +0.178 [-0.098,0.454] and crosses null. I reject and close them, and I defer the direct PIK3CA strata H129-H130 because the interaction did not establish modification. Overall, these results strengthen SMARCA4, KEAP1, and TP53 associations across protocol and co-loss contexts, add a validated-quality context for PTEN while maintaining appropriate caution near the threshold, and do not alter the restriction that all conclusions are dataset-internal associations rather than causal or externally generalizable claims.",
  "research_notes": "Core accepted associations: SMARCA4 loss -> stronger dependency; KEAP1 loss -> weaker dependency; TP53 loss -> weaker dependency; PTEN loss -> modest stronger dependency, strongest in clean-background adherent context and pending validation in clean-background high-quality adherent context. New co-loss background contrasts support broad persistence of SMARCA4, KEAP1, and TP53 effects; H134 TP53-with-SMARCA4 context remains uncertain because the interval is wide and lower bound is near null. Protocol probes media 10 vs 5 and Humagne library are null, reducing concern that major effects are driven by these measured culture/library differences. PTEN multiplicity is managed by deferring redundant near-threshold contexts and validating H126. PIK3CA interactions and direct PIK3CA strata are deferred because H120 was not supported. All claims are dataset-internal; do not infer causality, single-gene knockout specificity, or external generalizability.",
